// The 15:30 ruling, measured: a transform's argument has to be a function the program knows before it// runs, and the closure that captures a value is refused where it is written.//// The author's 15:30 review (docs/handover/reviews/questions-2026-10-29.md) fixes the end of the class// "被调者是参数或捕获": not "support the capture one day", but a compile error at the call, because the// transform builds the transformed graph right there, out of the graph of the function it is handed.//// Two shapes stand side by side here, and only one of them is refused:////   `by_param` -- `transpose(f)` where `f` is a parameter. The compiler instantiates the body per call//   site, so at `by_param(double)` the argument *is* `double` and the transpose is built: this answers//   2.0, the paper answer. The parameter only becomes a problem when it stays one, and that is what the//   standard library's own `vjp(f: F)` does at stdlib/vjp.cml#vjp -- which is why the parameter half of//   the refusal is declared off, with the measurement beside it (CallSpec:://   staticFunctionArgRefusesParameters). Turning it on refuses `vjp`'s body and eight `vjp_api` cases.////   `by_capture` -- `transpose(h)` where `h` is a closure holding `x`. Nothing here can name the graph://   the static function is known, but the captured value is not, so the transposed graph would have to//   take the capture as a parameter of its own. It is refused at the call, on the argument's line:////     error[SemanticDiag::TransformArgumentIsNotAKnownFunction]: 'transpose' is applied to a closure//     that captures a value, and a transform's argument has to be a function this program knows before//     it runs.//        --> .../transpose_arg_not_a_static_function.cml:15:26//         |//      15 |     let back = transpose(h)//         |                          ^//// Before the refusal existed this program *compiled* and then failed in the run-time half with// `TRANSPOSE function=__g_51__ skip=residual uri=:op/mul_d operand=1` -- a number a reader has to// decode, after the program has already started. The point of the error is that it is the same fact,// said where the mistake is.//// A function that is known by name and calls a closure *inside its body* is the other class// (transpose_no_tangent_closure.cml): its argument is a name, so nothing here refuses it, and the 15:30// review gives that one a different end -- the body's calls instantiate at compile time by recursion// into the called graph.import { transpose } from transformsimport rulesets.transposefunc double(x: float): float {    return 2.0 * x}func by_param(f: (x: float) => float): float {    let back = transpose(f)    return back(2.0, 1.0)}func by_capture(x: float): float {    let h = (y: float) => y * x    let back = transpose(h)    return back(1.0, 1.0)}func main(): int sync {    println(by_param(double))    println(by_capture(3.0))    return 0}